4. List three backup best practices for Windows Server that would be important for a Flipkart-style e-commerce website, and explain why each is critical for minimizing downtime or data loss.
=>Application-consistent (VSS-aware / database-aware) backups of the order, inventory, and payment databases
Why critical: A crash-consistent backup of a live SQL Server / MongoDB / etc. can leave transactions half-written. Application-consistent backups guarantee that the database can be restored to a transactionally consistent state, preventing order loss, double charges, or inventory corruption — any of which would cause immediate revenue and trust damage.
3-2-1 rule with off-site / immutable copies and short RPO
(At least 3 copies, on 2 different media, 1 off-site; ideally one copy immutable or air-gapped.)
Why critical: Ransomware, regional outages, or a compromised admin account can destroy local backups. An off-site or immutable copy ensures the business can still recover product catalogs, customer data, and recent orders even if the primary data center is lost, keeping downtime and data loss within acceptable RTO/RPO targets (often minutes to a few hours for e-commerce).
Regular automated restore testing + monitoring of backup success/failure
Why critical: A backup that has never been restored is only a hope. Automated restore drills (and alerting on failed backups) verify that recovery actually works within the required time window. For a high-traffic site, discovering a broken backup only during a real outage can mean hours of downtime, lost sales, and regulatory/compliance issues.
